#: ../../source/docs/common-electro-permanent-magnet-gripper.rst:175
#: 5e4cf7d23d264dcebd971bfa324877a6
msgid "Magnetic interference and Board Voltage check"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-electro-permanent-magnet-gripper.rst:177
#: 08028bc3c1094362bc68fbdd523c0e80
msgid "The EPM can cause magnetic interference especially while gripping or releasing the cargo or if left in the \"on\" state with no cargo attached.  Make sure to mount the EPM at least 10cm from the flight controller's compass."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-electro-permanent-magnet-gripper.rst:182
#: bc3ca51633534d778d07340e8ab96a66
msgid "The EPM's impact on the compass can be tested in real-time by connecting with the Mission Planner, go to the Flight Data screen and click on the \"Tuning\" checkbox at the bottom, middle.  Double click on the graph that appears on the top right and when the \"Graph This\" window appears select \"magfield\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-electro-permanent-magnet-gripper.rst:188
#: 925e37e3625a442a9f625244f14340df
msgid "Turning the EPM on and off and ensure the magfield length does not change by more than 10%."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-electro-permanent-magnet-gripper.rst:194
#: fe86a16ea8904d36b04a77e96fce8aaf
msgid "In addition to magnetic interference, if a weak BEC is used a voltage drop may be noticed.  For example the BEC in a 3DR ESC leads to a drop of 0.4V when the EPM is activated.  This is normally not a concern because the BEC should only be powering the servo rail of the autopilot, so the CPU's voltage should be unaffected.  This can be tested by graphing the \"hwvoltage\" field in the same way the \"magfield\" was checked above.  Ideally this test should be performed when connected via :ref:`Telemetry <common-telemetry-landingpage>`\\ because a USB cable will also provide power the CPU and may hide any voltage disturbance."
msgstr ""

